  <title>Assembled products</title>

  <p><guiseq><gui>Edit</gui><gui>Assembled products</gui></guiseq> keeps the
  bill of materials of each manufactured product: the parts, and how many of
  each, that go into one. Manufacturing uses it to take the parts out of
  stock. Changes are saved as you make them.</p>

  <steps>
    <item><p>Choose the manufactured product. <gui>Manufactured products</gui>
    opens the products window showing them, to add a new one.</p></item>
    <item><p>Choose the <gui>Version</gui> of its parts list.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click <gui>New Item</gui>, or press <key>F2</key>, and type the
    <gui>Qty</gui>, choose the <gui>Product</gui> and add a
    <gui>Remark</gui>. If the part is already on the list you are asked
    whether to update its quantity or add it again. The <gui>Qty</gui> can be
    a sum such as <input>2x12</input>; see
    <link xref="qty_arithmetic"/>.</p></item>
    <item><p><gui>Delete Item</gui>, or <key>F3</key>, removes the selected
    part.</p></item>
  </steps>

  <p>Each part shows its <gui>Cost</gui> and <gui>Retail</gui> price, and
  each multiplied by its quantity in <gui>Ext cost</gui> and <gui>Ext
  retail</gui>. Retail is the part's price at the standard markup level (see
  <link xref="customer_markup_percent"/>). Under the list, <gui>Total items
  :</gui>, <gui>Total Cost :</gui> and <gui>Parts retail :</gui> add up the
  list at the parts' current prices, and <gui>Product cost :</gui> and
  <gui>Product retail :</gui> are what the assembled product itself is saved
  at. <gui>Notes</gui> are kept with the version. Right click a part for its
  <gui>Product hub</gui>. You can also drag products onto the list.</p>

  <section id="update_prices">
  <title>Updating the product's cost and prices</title>
  <p>Once a version is chosen, <gui>Update cost &amp; prices</gui> sets the
  assembled product's cost and its selling prices, without opening the product
  itself.</p>
  <steps>
    <item><p>The <gui>Cost &amp; prices</gui> window shows the product's
    current cost and the parts total of the chosen version, and starts
    <gui>Update the cost to</gui> at that parts total. Change it to cover
    labor or anything else the parts do not.</p></item>
    <item><p>Each markup level keeps the markup its price makes now, so its
    <gui>Sell price</gui> follows the cost as you change it. A level with no
    price of its own yet starts at the level's markup. Type a <gui>Markup
    %</gui> or a <gui>Sell price</gui> to set a level yourself; a price cannot
    go below the cost.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click <gui>Update</gui> to save the cost and every level's price,
    or <gui>Cancel</gui> to change nothing.</p></item>
  </steps>
  <p>If the product is open in the product window on another computer, it
  cannot be updated until that window is closed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="versions">
  <title>Versions</title>
  <p>When a product's design changes, make a new version of its parts list
  rather than changing the old one, so what was made before still shows what
  it used. Click <gui>Version</gui> to open the <gui>Product Assembly
  Version</gui> window:</p>
  <list>
    <item><p>Type the <gui>Name</gui> of the new version. Check <gui>Use
    assembly items from version</gui> and choose one to start from a copy of
    its parts and notes. Click <gui>Create version</gui>.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click a version's name to rename it. Uncheck <gui>Active</gui> to
    retire it.</p></item>
  </list>
  </section>

  <section id="menu">
  <title>The window menu</title>
  <terms>
    <item><title><gui>Export list to CSV file</gui></title>
    <p>Saves the parts list to a spreadsheet file, with each part's order
    number from the vendor you choose, for ordering the parts.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Products</gui></title>
    <p>Opens the <link xref="products">products</link> window.</p></item>
  </terms>
  </section>

